WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
The customer has chosen.

2
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:02.920
The price is right.

3
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.920
The hard part should be over.

4
00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:08.247
Then the checkout button never receives keyboard focus.

5
00:00:08.487 --> 00:00:10.517
For the merchant, a sale stops.

6
00:00:10.947 --> 00:00:13.707
For the customer, independence stops with it.

7
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.540
Can I Finish helps teams find that moment, and

8
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.500
fix the journey.

9
00:00:17.937 --> 00:00:20.817
Meet Field Notes: a digital notebook with an actual

10
00:00:20.837 --> 00:00:22.757
download. One product.

11
00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.350
One dollar in PayPal sandbox funds.

12
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.530
A simple purchase with a clear finish line: reach

13
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.290
checkout, approve the order, and receive the notebook.

14
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.590
This is where our accessibility workflow meets a working

15
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.630
payment integration.

16
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:38.343
First, the merchant control before the repair.

17
00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:39.563
It looks ready.

18
00:00:40.003 --> 00:00:42.363
But eighteen Tab presses pass it by.

19
00:00:42.823 --> 00:00:45.823
Can I Finish records each key, the focused element,

20
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:47.063
and the screenshot.

21
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:50.383
The report identifies a div with a click handler

22
00:00:50.403 --> 00:00:53.663
where the customer needs a keyboard-operable button.

23
00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:57.492
The on-device AI explains the supplied finding and proposes

24
00:00:57.512 --> 00:00:58.532
a native button.

25
00:00:58.759 --> 00:01:00.139
The developer makes that change.

26
00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:02.859
Then the same browser contract runs again.

27
00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:05.966
Four key events later, the corrected checkout opens PayPal.

28
00:01:06.526 --> 00:01:08.086
Same task. Same product.

29
00:01:08.446 --> 00:01:10.266
A finish line we can actually verify.

30
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.120
Under the hood, the PayPal Orders API creates the

31
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.300
order and sends the buyer to approval.

32
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.540
Here is an actual sandbox purchase: order completed, capture

33
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.540
completed, one dollar verified.

34
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.080
The server checks the amount and status before showing

35
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.460
the receipt. Field Notes is ready to download.

36
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.720
The payment has evidence, too.

37
00:01:31.106 --> 00:01:32.086
Change your mind?

38
00:01:32.586 --> 00:01:34.326
Cancellation brings you back to the store.

39
00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:36.646
Ready again? Start checkout again.

40
00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:39.706
Signed sessions bind the order to the journey, and

41
00:01:39.726 --> 00:01:42.686
idempotent capture handling protects retries.

42
00:01:42.912 --> 00:01:45.652
The merchant gets a practical integration, with the keyboard

43
00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.052
evidence beside it.

44
00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:52.073
Can I Finish brings browser evidence, AI-assisted repair proposals,

45
00:01:52.213 --> 00:01:55.113
and repeatable verification to the moment a customer wants

46
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.693
to finish. Export the regression test.

47
00:01:57.993 --> 00:01:59.913
Keep the repair in the next release.

48
00:02:00.593 --> 00:02:02.853
Because a better checkout starts with making the last

49
00:02:02.873 --> 00:02:04.053
step reachable.